\caption(Explicit 8-epoch Adam fit and a tensor-computed true/predicted-class count matrix at the selected checkpoint. Training uses augmentation and row branch dropout; validation/test use full branches. LayerNorm has no moving state. Counts sum to 64; IDs follow the original CIFAR-10 order.)
\alttext(Explicit 8-epoch Adam fit and a tensor-computed true/predicted-class count matrix at the selected checkpoint. Training uses augmentation and row branch dropout; validation/test use full branches. LayerNorm has no moving state. Counts sum to 64; IDs follow the original CIFAR-10 order.)


> Observed subset result

Validation selects epoch 4: validation NLL 2.127380, inference training NLL
1.889064, test NLL 2.110236 and 23.4375% accuracy on 64 held-out test images.
This small fixed-seed result does not establish a V1/V2 accuracy ranking or reproduce
published ImageNet performance.

> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

\image(figures/convnext_v2_confusion_api_0.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 1. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)


> Conclusion

Controlled tensor fixtures explain the residual rule, while exact count formulas and actual
Atto/Tiny forwards distinguish architecture budgets from large allocations. Visible optimization
connects the complete Atto topology to data without claiming published pretraining or benchmarks.

> References

- [Woo et al. (2023), ConvNeXt V2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.00808).
- [ConvNeXt V2 release](https://github.com/facebookresearch/ConvNeXt-V2).
- [CIFAR-10 dataset](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html).
